   "description": "The pen-test report states that anonymous users can read sensitive operational data from the\nservice's management endpoints, including **plain-text database credentials**.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `GET /actuator` — lists about 20 links (`env`, `beans`, `configprops`, `mappings`, `loggers`, …).\n2. `GET /actuator/env` — search the response for `password`:\n   ```json\n   \"spring.datasource.password\": { \"value\": \"Local-Dev-Passw0rd!\", ... }\n   ```\n3. `GET /actuator/health` — returns database type and disk space details to an anonymous caller.\n4. `POST /actuator/loggers/com.acme.commerce` with `{\"configuredLevel\":\"TRACE\"}` — anyone can change log levels.\n\n## Expected result\n\n* Only `health`, `info` and `metrics` are exposed over HTTP.\n* No endpoint ever returns a secret value.\n* Anonymous `GET /actuator/health` returns only `{\"status\":\"UP\"}`. Liveness/readiness probes keep working\n  (`/actuator/health/liveness`, `/actuator/health/readiness`) — Kubernetes depends on them.\n\n## Actual result\n\nAs in steps above.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Exposure limited to `health`, `info`, `metrics`.\n- [ ] `/actuator/env` returns `404`.\n- [ ] Health details hidden from anonymous callers; probes still return `200`.\n- [ ] Automated test proving `/actuator/env` is not reachable.\n- [ ] PR explains why \"it's only DEV\" is not an acceptable reason to leave this as is.\n\n## Security note\n\nDo not paste real secrets from any environment into Jira, Slack or the PR.\n\n## References\n\nBusiness rules **SEC-1**, **SEC-2**, **SEC-3**.",
