   "description": "## User story\n\n**As the** business\n**I want** stock updates to be safe under concurrent orders\n**so that** we never sell more units than we have.\n\n## Background\n\nDuring the Black Friday load test, 2 orders were accepted for a product with 1 unit in stock.\nBoth requests read `stock = 1`, both decremented, the last write won, and stock ended at 0 while\ntwo units were sold.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Two concurrent orders for the last unit: exactly one succeeds (`201`), the other gets\n   `409 Conflict` with `errorCode: CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION` (the client can retry).\n2. Updating a product (PUT) at the same time as an order does not lose either change.\n3. A test demonstrates the conflict (e.g. two threads, or two transactions updating the same\n   loaded entity version).\n4. A Flyway migration adds what the database needs. Existing rows must keep working.\n\n## Technical notes\n\n* Research JPA optimistic locking (`@Version`) vs pessimistic locking\n  (`@Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)`). Write a short comparison in the PR and justify your choice.\n* `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException` must not surface as a `500`.",
